1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,580
are going to be above average, half will be below average.

2
00:00:02,580 --> 00:00:04,960
So this is not a market about consumers, on average,

3
00:00:04,960 --> 00:00:06,120
getting ripped off.

4
00:00:06,120 --> 00:00:08,080
It's a model where half the customers

5
00:00:08,080 --> 00:00:09,600
get less than they bargain for, half

6
00:00:09,600 --> 00:00:11,360
get more than they bargain for.

7
00:00:11,360 --> 00:00:13,560
Does that sound like a terrible market to be in?

8
00:00:13,560 --> 00:00:15,840
Well, 50-50 chance of getting more than I thought or less

9
00:00:15,840 --> 00:00:17,520
than I thought.

10
00:00:17,520 --> 00:00:20,000
That sounds like any other market

11
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:22,960
where there's a symmetric distribution of outcomes.

12
00:00:22,960 --> 00:00:26,480
So no particular reason to be dissatisfied in the market

13
00:00:26,480 --> 00:00:28,680
if you, in fact, buy.

14
00:00:28,680 --> 00:00:30,840
So what's going wrong with these markets?

15
00:00:30,840 --> 00:00:33,160
The problem with the markets is all the exchanges

16
00:00:33,160 --> 00:00:34,800
that don't happen.

17
00:00:34,800 --> 00:00:38,120
It's not a market about the rip-offs that we see,

18
00:00:38,120 --> 00:00:39,880
but about the mutually beneficial trades

19
00:00:39,880 --> 00:00:41,280
that we don't see.

20
00:00:41,280 --> 00:00:44,200
Remember Bastiat, what is seen and what is not seen.

21
00:00:44,200 --> 00:00:46,280
In the adverse selection model, the problem

22
00:00:46,280 --> 00:00:49,200
is all the cars that actually don't get

23
00:00:49,200 --> 00:00:52,200
sold because they're too good.

24
00:00:52,200 --> 00:00:53,780
It's the people with the better cars,

25
00:00:53,780 --> 00:00:55,520
they're not going to go and sell that car

26
00:00:55,520 --> 00:00:58,600
and get that stupid low price, even though they actually

27
00:00:58,600 --> 00:01:00,040
don't really want the car that much,

28
00:01:00,040 --> 00:01:01,240
even though there's a buyer out there

29
00:01:01,240 --> 00:01:04,360
who, with perfect information, would totally buy that car.

30
00:01:04,360 --> 00:01:06,600
But due to the adverse selection problem,

31
00:01:06,600 --> 00:01:09,080
because the best stuff gets pulled off the market,

32
00:01:09,080 --> 00:01:10,520
the trades don't happen.

33
00:01:10,520 --> 00:01:12,360
Same thing with health insurance.

34
00:01:12,360 --> 00:01:14,480
The problem of the adverse selection problem

35
00:01:14,480 --> 00:01:16,680
implies not that you're ripped off

36
00:01:16,680 --> 00:01:18,360
if you buy health insurance.

37
00:01:18,360 --> 00:01:22,240
The problem is all the people who would like health insurance,

38
00:01:22,240 --> 00:01:27,400
but who nevertheless don't wind up buying it because they

39
00:01:27,400 --> 00:01:30,480
are the healthier people and they pull out of the market.

40
00:01:30,480 --> 00:01:33,640
So the idea is that if you're a healthy person

41
00:01:33,640 --> 00:01:36,080
and you could receive insurance that was accurately

42
00:01:36,080 --> 00:01:37,880
priced at a risk, then you would just

43
00:01:37,880 --> 00:01:39,240
go and get super cheap insurance.

44
00:01:39,240 --> 00:01:41,160
And you'd be happy saying, hey, I'm super healthy,

45
00:01:41,160 --> 00:01:42,740
but I love having insurance because it

46
00:01:42,740 --> 00:01:44,960
gives me peace of mind at a really low price

47
00:01:44,960 --> 00:01:46,720
because I'm so healthy.

48
00:01:46,720 --> 00:01:48,400
And the problem with adverse selection

49
00:01:48,400 --> 00:01:51,440
is those people don't buy insurance.

50
00:01:51,440 --> 00:01:55,320
And therefore, the gains to trade for the healthy people

51
00:01:55,320 --> 00:01:57,740
to buying insurance go out and disappear.

52
00:01:57,740 --> 00:01:59,400
Right now, of course, in the real world,

53
00:01:59,400 --> 00:02:00,840
when people complain about health insurance,

54
00:02:00,840 --> 00:02:02,840
the complaint is always that what kind of people

55
00:02:02,840 --> 00:02:04,320
can't get insurance?

56
00:02:04,320 --> 00:02:05,640
Sick people.

57
00:02:05,640 --> 00:02:08,120
That's not the adverse selection model at all.

58
00:02:08,120 --> 00:02:10,040
Complete opposite.

59
00:02:10,040 --> 00:02:12,360
The problem in the adverse selection problem

60
00:02:12,360 --> 00:02:14,160
would be that healthy people aren't getting insurance.

61
00:02:14,160 --> 00:02:15,280
Have you ever heard someone saying,

62
00:02:15,280 --> 00:02:17,600
I'm just so sad about all the healthy people who

63
00:02:17,600 --> 00:02:20,800
don't buy insurance because their health is so good that it's

64
00:02:20,800 --> 00:02:22,920
not worth it for them to pay these prices inflated

65
00:02:22,920 --> 00:02:24,560
by all the sick people?

66
00:02:24,560 --> 00:02:27,520
Is anyone going and having a day of sorrow

67
00:02:27,520 --> 00:02:30,560
for these healthy people who don't buy insurance?

68
00:02:30,560 --> 00:02:31,600
Yeah, very unusual.

69
00:02:31,600 --> 00:02:32,100
Yeah, Ash?

70
00:02:32,100 --> 00:02:33,480
Is this Jonathan Kruger?

71
00:02:33,480 --> 00:02:34,480
Yeah, Kruger.

72
00:02:34,480 --> 00:02:35,840
Yes.

73
00:02:35,840 --> 00:02:36,640
Well, let's see.

74
00:02:36,640 --> 00:02:37,720
I read his graphic novel.

75
00:02:37,720 --> 00:02:40,840
He didn't have any pages in the graphic novel shedding tears

76
00:02:40,840 --> 00:02:42,520
for them.

77
00:02:42,520 --> 00:02:44,120
In fact, in the graphic novel, so I

78
00:02:44,120 --> 00:02:46,200
wrote a review of the graphic novel on the theory

79
00:02:46,200 --> 00:02:48,600
that if you write it, it can be reviewed.

80
00:02:48,600 --> 00:02:50,600
And in the graphic novel, what he says

81
00:02:50,600 --> 00:02:56,040
is that, well, some young people don't buy it

82
00:02:56,040 --> 00:03:00,640
because they don't realize they could get sick too.

83
00:03:00,640 --> 00:03:03,000
That's one possible story.

84
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:05,920
It's not a story that any economist else in the world

85
00:03:05,920 --> 00:03:06,760
is telling.

86
00:03:06,760 --> 00:03:08,220
It's like, well, there's the problem

87
00:03:08,220 --> 00:03:10,600
that young people believe they have zero probability

88
00:03:10,600 --> 00:03:12,080
of getting sick.

89
00:03:12,080 --> 00:03:14,280
It's like quite an error for people to be making.

90
00:03:17,560 --> 00:03:18,440
So why did he do that?

91
00:03:18,440 --> 00:03:20,600
Well, I believe that he was trying

92
00:03:20,600 --> 00:03:23,100
to give an argument that would be persuasive rather than one

93
00:03:23,100 --> 00:03:24,840
that would be strictly accurate.

94
00:03:24,840 --> 00:03:27,000
So at least that's my best story.

95
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:29,600
I think that he would understand the model more than well

96
00:03:29,600 --> 00:03:30,960
enough to not make the mistake.

97
00:03:30,960 --> 00:03:31,800
Yes?

98
00:03:31,800 --> 00:03:34,840
Yeah, if it's the people who are healthy above average

99
00:03:34,840 --> 00:03:38,480
in the model that don't get satisfied and then it unravels,

100
00:03:38,480 --> 00:03:41,120
it also means that people who are unhealthy also

101
00:03:41,120 --> 00:03:41,620
don't get it.

102
00:03:41,620 --> 00:03:42,120
Yes, yes.

103
00:03:42,120 --> 00:03:44,320
So yeah, you could say the problem is that only the,

104
00:03:44,320 --> 00:03:47,640
but again, still, the complaint is that the sickest people

105
00:03:47,640 --> 00:03:48,680
don't get it.

106
00:03:48,680 --> 00:03:51,760
And no matter how, unless there's 100% unraveling,

107
00:03:51,760 --> 00:03:54,520
it's always the less sick people that are not buying.

108
00:03:54,520 --> 00:03:58,760
So essentially, the people that people are most worried about,

109
00:03:58,760 --> 00:04:01,560
most sad about, are the ones the model says

110
00:04:01,560 --> 00:04:03,200
will buy the insurance.

111
00:04:03,200 --> 00:04:05,560
And it's everybody else that actually is the problem

112
00:04:05,560 --> 00:04:08,480
according to the model.

113
00:04:08,480 --> 00:04:09,520
OK.

114
00:04:09,520 --> 00:04:10,120
All right.

115
00:04:10,120 --> 00:04:11,160
So anyway, the efficiency problem

116
00:04:11,160 --> 00:04:14,040
stands for exchanges that don't happen because buyers can't

117
00:04:14,040 --> 00:04:17,160
distinguish good cars from bad and health insurance companies

118
00:04:17,160 --> 00:04:18,800
can't distinguish the really sick people

119
00:04:18,800 --> 00:04:20,720
from the really healthy people.

120
00:04:20,720 --> 00:04:22,320
Now, here's the fun thing.

121
00:04:24,800 --> 00:04:26,600
So in a world of perfect information,

122
00:04:26,600 --> 00:04:29,880
what would happen in the insurance market?

123
00:04:29,880 --> 00:04:31,320
Because there'd be no insurance.

124
00:04:31,320 --> 00:04:32,820
Everyone would just be charged based

125
00:04:32,820 --> 00:04:35,160
upon their actual costs.

126
00:04:35,160 --> 00:04:39,520
Now, how about in a world of symmetric information

127
00:04:39,520 --> 00:04:41,120
where you know your general riskiness,

128
00:04:41,120 --> 00:04:44,560
but you don't know the actual details of how bad things will

129
00:04:44,560 --> 00:04:46,480
be?

130
00:04:46,480 --> 00:04:50,400
So in other words, imagine that you have a risk category, which

131
00:04:50,400 --> 00:04:54,000
you know, A through F. But then within the A category,

132
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:56,400
there's another random variable that gets tacked on.

133
00:04:56,400 --> 00:04:58,160
Within the B category, there's another random variable

134
00:04:58,160 --> 00:04:59,280
that tacks on.

135
00:04:59,280 --> 00:05:03,880
So in that case, what would the pricing of LBLF?

136
00:05:03,880 --> 00:05:07,040
So insurance companies, they know your grade of health,

137
00:05:07,040 --> 00:05:09,720
but nobody knows this random variable, which literally

138
00:05:09,720 --> 00:05:13,120
has to be you have to throw the dice every year or whatever.

139
00:05:13,120 --> 00:05:15,440
So in a world like that, what does the health insurance

140
00:05:15,440 --> 00:05:17,920
market look like?

141
00:05:17,920 --> 00:05:20,480
Is there any health insurance sold then?

142
00:05:20,480 --> 00:05:22,800
Would it be according to your expected health?

143
00:05:22,800 --> 00:05:23,480
Yeah, exactly.

144
00:05:23,480 --> 00:05:26,640
So you would have a charger rate based upon your health grade.

145
00:05:26,640 --> 00:05:28,520
And then why do you want to buy insurance?

146
00:05:28,520 --> 00:05:30,800
Because you want to insure against that random variable

147
00:05:30,800 --> 00:05:31,680
part.

148
00:05:31,680 --> 00:05:34,160
So yeah, I'm A health.

149
00:05:34,160 --> 00:05:35,200
That's what I know.

150
00:05:35,200 --> 00:05:36,280
That's what insurance company knows.

151
00:05:36,280 --> 00:05:39,640
But I could get hit by a bus, and then I'd have a problem.

152
00:05:39,640 --> 00:05:42,000
So and again, of course, that A grade

153
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:46,880
should in a world without asymmetric information

154
00:05:46,880 --> 00:05:50,440
would include knowledge of the riskiness of your behavior.

155
00:05:50,440 --> 00:05:53,400
So how often do you look before you cross the street?

156
00:05:53,400 --> 00:05:54,560
Are you a careful looker?

157
00:05:54,560 --> 00:05:56,200
Are you three way?

158
00:05:56,200 --> 00:05:57,360
Or do you look both way?

159
00:05:57,360 --> 00:05:58,880
Look left, right, left again?

160
00:05:58,880 --> 00:06:00,160
Or what do you do?

161
00:06:00,160 --> 00:06:01,520
Are you a skydiver?

162
00:06:01,520 --> 00:06:03,720
What's your diet like?

163
00:06:03,720 --> 00:06:08,000
So and then, yeah, so in a world where nobody had any clue

164
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:09,800
at all about their health, suppose

165
00:06:09,800 --> 00:06:11,560
nobody had any idea what their health was,

166
00:06:11,560 --> 00:06:13,880
and the insurance company doesn't know, you don't know,

167
00:06:13,880 --> 00:06:17,400
then is there going to be any insurance in that world?

168
00:06:17,400 --> 00:06:17,920
Sure.

169
00:06:17,920 --> 00:06:19,640
And what do the rates look like in a world

170
00:06:19,640 --> 00:06:22,000
where nobody has any clue about anything?

171
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:25,120
Yeah, then everyone just pays the average rate.

172
00:06:25,120 --> 00:06:28,040
All right, so what I think people really want

173
00:06:28,040 --> 00:06:31,440
is a world where we pretend that everyone's average.

174
00:06:31,440 --> 00:06:32,900
That's the dream that people have.

175
00:06:32,900 --> 00:06:36,040
And then everyone gets charged the same rate.

176
00:06:36,040 --> 00:06:37,440
Or of course, there's some people

177
00:06:37,440 --> 00:06:39,320
have a dream of enforcing it by regulation

178
00:06:39,320 --> 00:06:41,200
that everyone must be treated the same.

179
00:06:41,200 --> 00:06:44,440
So Charles Murray, in his book on the universal basic income,

180
00:06:44,440 --> 00:06:46,280
actually advocates a regulation saying

181
00:06:46,280 --> 00:06:49,480
that literally everyone, every adult in the country

182
00:06:49,480 --> 00:06:51,640
must be sold insurance at the same rate,

183
00:06:51,640 --> 00:06:53,240
not even adjusting for health.

184
00:06:53,240 --> 00:06:56,760
Not even adjusting for age.

185
00:06:56,760 --> 00:07:00,480
And but anyway, so this is the idea.

186
00:07:00,480 --> 00:07:02,760
Now, of course, in the real world,

187
00:07:02,760 --> 00:07:04,040
you pass a law like that.

188
00:07:04,040 --> 00:07:05,000
What does every health insurance?

189
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:07,040
So if you basically say you must treat everyone

190
00:07:07,040 --> 00:07:09,600
as if they're identically risky, even though everybody knows

191
00:07:09,600 --> 00:07:11,480
they're not, so what does every health insurance

192
00:07:11,480 --> 00:07:14,040
company try to do in a world like this Charles Murray world?

193
00:07:14,040 --> 00:07:17,240
Drive the price up as high as they can.

194
00:07:17,240 --> 00:07:19,400
Well, if you drive the price up as high as you can,

195
00:07:19,400 --> 00:07:25,160
that by itself will, you know, so let's see.

196
00:07:25,160 --> 00:07:26,760
What's the right way?

197
00:07:26,760 --> 00:07:28,520
So that by itself doesn't actually,

198
00:07:28,520 --> 00:07:32,920
so that'll just mean that everyone will want to buy

199
00:07:32,920 --> 00:07:34,400
from the cheaper people.

200
00:07:34,400 --> 00:07:37,040
So by raising your price higher than everybody else

201
00:07:37,040 --> 00:07:41,080
doesn't actually differentially attract healthier people.

202
00:07:41,080 --> 00:07:42,160
But yeah, Nathan?

203
00:07:42,160 --> 00:07:44,680
About the services you offer, so if I don't have, say,

204
00:07:44,680 --> 00:07:46,360
a cancer provider, then someone like me

205
00:07:46,360 --> 00:07:49,000
with high risk of cancer, I'm not going to buy that insurance.

206
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:50,440
Yeah, so you know, how about this?

207
00:07:50,440 --> 00:07:52,640
You just have like tons of like sports medicine

208
00:07:52,640 --> 00:07:55,760
and other stuff for like super physically active people,

209
00:07:55,760 --> 00:07:56,260
right?

210
00:07:56,260 --> 00:07:59,320
So you know, like we have basically a bunch of services

211
00:07:59,320 --> 00:08:01,440
that only young and healthy people would ever want,

212
00:08:01,440 --> 00:08:03,160
and then you just don't do anything else.

213
00:08:03,160 --> 00:08:05,000
So Murray didn't really deal with this,

214
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:08,040
but that is the idea.

215
00:08:08,040 --> 00:08:12,720
All right, so when you actually think

216
00:08:12,720 --> 00:08:15,160
about what it is people are complaining about,

217
00:08:15,160 --> 00:08:18,560
the problem isn't really that it's a world where people are

218
00:08:18,560 --> 00:08:22,880
not being priced, so yeah, the real problem is that people,

219
00:08:22,880 --> 00:08:26,560
is that prices are not adequately tailored to risk.

220
00:08:26,560 --> 00:08:28,720
But the populist complaint about insurance

221
00:08:28,720 --> 00:08:30,480
has nothing to do with that at all.

222
00:08:30,480 --> 00:08:32,000
In fact, quite the opposite.

223
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:34,120
I think most people's nightmare world

224
00:08:34,120 --> 00:08:36,640
is that everyone has insurance that is exactly

225
00:08:36,640 --> 00:08:39,400
tailored to your personal risk rate, right?

226
00:08:39,400 --> 00:08:42,640
And again, imagine a world where you actually

227
00:08:42,640 --> 00:08:45,120
are simultaneously solving adverse selection and moral

228
00:08:45,120 --> 00:08:50,520
hazard by say, charging people based upon their BMI.

229
00:08:50,520 --> 00:08:52,640
Charging people based upon like you monitor

230
00:08:52,640 --> 00:08:53,800
how often you go to the gym.

231
00:08:53,800 --> 00:08:55,920
You have a bunch of different measures, right?

232
00:08:55,920 --> 00:08:58,240
And go and charge people for all of these details

233
00:08:58,240 --> 00:09:00,680
about their actual riskiness.

234
00:09:00,680 --> 00:09:03,400
This is the kind of thing that would infuriate normal people.

235
00:09:03,400 --> 00:09:05,680
But economically, that's much better.

236
00:09:05,680 --> 00:09:08,440
That actually solves the problem.

237
00:09:08,440 --> 00:09:09,200
It actually is.

238
00:09:09,200 --> 00:09:10,920
That's solving the moral hazard problem

239
00:09:10,920 --> 00:09:12,660
and solving the adverse selection problem,

240
00:09:12,660 --> 00:09:15,560
just tailor rates to risk, and then there's no longer

241
00:09:15,560 --> 00:09:17,400
any problem.

242
00:09:17,400 --> 00:09:19,360
All right, now, so adverse selection

243
00:09:19,360 --> 00:09:21,120
is probably economists' favorite argument

244
00:09:21,120 --> 00:09:23,200
for insurance regulation.

245
00:09:23,200 --> 00:09:26,040
Moral hazard is often thrown in there,

246
00:09:26,040 --> 00:09:27,520
even though there are essentially

247
00:09:27,520 --> 00:09:31,640
no insurance regulations that do anything about moral hazard.

248
00:09:31,640 --> 00:09:33,240
If you really start thinking about it,

249
00:09:33,240 --> 00:09:38,240
then you have a few things like jail time for insurance fraud.

250
00:09:38,240 --> 00:09:41,520
All right, so that's a pretty good counter example.

