00:10:13,280 --> 00:10:16,520
All right, so, well, Double Indemnity,

267
00:10:16,520 --> 00:10:19,800
the title refers to insurance policies, which maybe still

268
00:10:19,800 --> 00:10:22,120
exist, but anyway, at the time in the 40s and 50s,

269
00:10:22,120 --> 00:10:24,080
there's a lot of insurance policies

270
00:10:24,080 --> 00:10:27,200
that would specify that you get double the payoff if people

271
00:10:27,200 --> 00:10:32,640
die in a rare and gruesome way, such as in a train accident.

272
00:10:32,640 --> 00:10:34,920
Then there's a woman who hates her husband,

273
00:10:34,920 --> 00:10:38,360
and she conspires with an insurance agent

274
00:10:38,360 --> 00:10:41,600
to go and sell her a very large policy with a double indemnity,

275
00:10:41,600 --> 00:10:43,880
and then gets him to murder her husband,

276
00:10:43,880 --> 00:10:46,360
and then they're going to fall in love and split the money,

277
00:10:46,360 --> 00:10:46,880
right?

278
00:10:46,880 --> 00:10:50,200
And then that's the way all these movies work out, right?

279
00:10:50,200 --> 00:10:52,880
Happily Ever After is the way that they all work out.

280
00:10:52,880 --> 00:10:57,120
But anyway, so if there were no law against going

281
00:10:57,120 --> 00:11:00,320
and trying to murder, getting an insurance policy on someone

282
00:11:00,320 --> 00:11:02,760
and then murdering them, so not only would this

283
00:11:02,760 --> 00:11:06,360
lead to more murders, right, but also would mess up the life

284
00:11:06,360 --> 00:11:08,320
insurance policy for the non-murderers,

285
00:11:08,320 --> 00:11:10,280
because you'd have a whole bunch of people say,

286
00:11:10,280 --> 00:11:12,920
oh, I want like a $50 million life insurance

287
00:11:12,920 --> 00:11:17,480
policy on my beloved enemy.

288
00:11:17,480 --> 00:11:19,800
And then you go and you take out a life insurance policy

289
00:11:19,800 --> 00:11:21,640
on someone you want dead.

290
00:11:21,640 --> 00:11:23,800
Or of course, it doesn't have to be

291
00:11:23,800 --> 00:11:25,200
that you're going to murder them.

292
00:11:25,200 --> 00:11:27,620
You might go and say, I'm just going to take out insurance

293
00:11:27,620 --> 00:11:31,160
policy on a friend that I know who's actually

294
00:11:31,160 --> 00:11:35,040
going to go and fight in Syria or something like that.

295
00:11:35,040 --> 00:11:37,240
I'm going to take out insurance policy on them.

296
00:11:37,240 --> 00:11:40,360
And if you can go and take out insurance policy on someone

297
00:11:40,360 --> 00:11:43,000
that you know is taking a lot of risks,

298
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:44,500
then again, there's a bunch of people

299
00:11:44,500 --> 00:11:46,520
that are buying the policies because they

300
00:11:46,520 --> 00:11:48,040
expect to get a high payoff.

301
00:11:48,040 --> 00:11:50,380
And if they get to buy it for the same rate as everybody

302
00:11:50,380 --> 00:11:52,840
else, then there's a problem.

303
00:11:52,840 --> 00:11:55,360
So anyway, adverse selections probably become his favorite

304
00:11:55,360 --> 00:11:57,240
argument for insurance regulation,

305
00:11:57,240 --> 00:12:00,760
and especially for one kind of regulation,

306
00:12:00,760 --> 00:12:05,760
which is the mandated purchase, a law that

307
00:12:05,760 --> 00:12:07,560
requires you to buy insurance.

308
00:12:07,560 --> 00:12:08,640
You must buy insurance.

309
00:12:08,640 --> 00:12:09,400
Yeah, Ash?

310
00:12:09,400 --> 00:12:12,440
I was just going to say, if insurance fraud would

311
00:12:12,440 --> 00:12:18,400
be legal, wouldn't that create a market for rating insurance

312
00:12:18,400 --> 00:12:19,480
purchasers?

313
00:12:19,480 --> 00:12:22,440
Sure, but given that's legal right now,

314
00:12:22,440 --> 00:12:25,400
so it's very reasonable to say that insurance companies would

315
00:12:25,400 --> 00:12:27,600
try to compensate for the problems.

316
00:12:27,600 --> 00:12:30,120
But still, it seems unlikely they'd

317
00:12:30,120 --> 00:12:32,440
be able to compensate as well because insurance companies

318
00:12:32,440 --> 00:12:33,240
can't jail anybody.

319
00:12:33,240 --> 00:12:35,400
Remember what I said, you can't contractually

320
00:12:35,400 --> 00:12:37,120
agree to private punishments.

321
00:12:37,120 --> 00:12:39,840
So yeah, you can imagine saying, I signed the insurance contract,

322
00:12:39,840 --> 00:12:41,640
and if I'm found guilty of insurance fraud,

323
00:12:41,640 --> 00:12:43,960
you can go and take everything I own.

324
00:12:43,960 --> 00:12:45,840
Just based on credit.

325
00:12:45,840 --> 00:12:50,160
Yeah, so but yes, but if the harsher penalties

326
00:12:50,160 --> 00:12:52,520
are unavailable to private organizations,

327
00:12:52,520 --> 00:12:54,200
but government has them, if government

328
00:12:54,200 --> 00:12:56,200
stops providing them, then probably

329
00:12:56,200 --> 00:12:59,480
it's going to be hard for the other alternatives

330
00:12:59,480 --> 00:13:00,280
to be as effective.

331
00:13:00,280 --> 00:13:02,880
Although, maybe they would be.

332
00:13:02,880 --> 00:13:07,040
So adverse selection is a very common rationale

333
00:13:07,040 --> 00:13:10,080
economists have for a law requiring everybody

334
00:13:10,080 --> 00:13:12,040
to buy insurance, which of course

335
00:13:12,040 --> 00:13:15,120
would be for car insurance or for health insurance.

336
00:13:15,120 --> 00:13:17,560
Anytime the law says you have to have insurance,

337
00:13:17,560 --> 00:13:20,880
there is a very simple story behind it

338
00:13:20,880 --> 00:13:24,640
that economists have, which is adverse selection.

339
00:13:24,640 --> 00:13:26,680
So again, in this case, imagine there

340
00:13:26,680 --> 00:13:28,520
were laws saying you must sell your company.

341
00:13:28,520 --> 00:13:30,920
You can't keep your company.

342
00:13:30,920 --> 00:13:33,880
So in that case, everyone sells their company,

343
00:13:33,880 --> 00:13:36,120
even though they know that the companies are actually

344
00:13:36,120 --> 00:13:39,120
worth more than what they're selling, or some of them.

345
00:13:39,120 --> 00:13:40,840
So in that case, all are sold.

346
00:13:40,840 --> 00:13:42,680
So all companies from 0 to 100 are sold,

347
00:13:42,680 --> 00:13:45,360
and the average value to the buyers is 50.

348
00:13:45,360 --> 00:13:47,520
And then sellers will be happy to buy,

349
00:13:47,520 --> 00:13:49,440
because they know that on average,

350
00:13:49,440 --> 00:13:55,320
they get a company that's worth 50 to the seller, 75 to them.

351
00:13:55,320 --> 00:13:57,280
And again, the interesting thing is

352
00:13:57,280 --> 00:14:01,440
that a lot of the sellers gain, although of course not all.

353
00:14:01,440 --> 00:14:04,560
Who are the sellers who lose as a result of the mandate?

354
00:14:04,560 --> 00:14:06,160
Sellers whose company is going to win.

355
00:14:06,160 --> 00:14:08,160
What's that?

356
00:14:08,160 --> 00:14:09,160
Well, let's see.

357
00:14:09,160 --> 00:14:11,840
Well, so let's see.

358
00:14:11,840 --> 00:14:12,480
Yes, yes, yes.

359
00:14:12,480 --> 00:14:14,520
So the story that I told, yes, then exactly.

360
00:14:14,520 --> 00:14:16,720
So all these people lose.

361
00:14:16,720 --> 00:14:19,240
So then how is there an efficiency gain?

362
00:14:19,240 --> 00:14:21,640
Well, the answer is when you add up the gains to all

363
00:14:21,640 --> 00:14:26,080
of these people, plus the profits to the buyers,

364
00:14:26,080 --> 00:14:28,480
it exceeds the losses to the sellers.

365
00:14:28,480 --> 00:14:32,160
And that is our definition of which kind of efficiency?

366
00:14:32,160 --> 00:14:32,800
Gallery Hicks.

367
00:14:32,800 --> 00:14:33,840
Gallery Hicks.

368
00:14:33,840 --> 00:14:35,560
Right, yes.

369
00:14:35,560 --> 00:14:39,240
If it's mandatory to sell at the price offered,

370
00:14:39,240 --> 00:14:42,720
why wouldn't the buyers offer a lower price?

371
00:14:42,720 --> 00:14:44,220
So there could be competition there.

372
00:14:44,220 --> 00:14:47,720
So basically, the buyers bid for a car.

373
00:14:47,720 --> 00:14:50,200
And with perfect competition, you

374
00:14:50,200 --> 00:14:52,600
think they would actually bid the price up to $75.

375
00:14:52,600 --> 00:14:54,040
And then actually even more gain.

376
00:14:54,040 --> 00:14:57,560
In that case, all of these people gain.

377
00:14:57,560 --> 00:15:00,560
On average, the buyers have no profit

378
00:15:00,560 --> 00:15:03,600
because they're going to be paying $75 for companies that

379
00:15:03,600 --> 00:15:05,520
are worth $175 to them.

380
00:15:05,520 --> 00:15:09,440
And then everything comes out of the hides of these people.

381
00:15:09,440 --> 00:15:11,160
But they don't even lose that much.

382
00:15:11,160 --> 00:15:14,920
That's the amazing thing.

383
00:15:14,920 --> 00:15:21,600
So adverse selection, standard justification for a mandate.

384
00:15:21,600 --> 00:15:24,240
Now, if you're wondering, what is

385
00:15:24,240 --> 00:15:28,560
the deal with all the other parts of things like Obamacare?

386
00:15:28,560 --> 00:15:30,120
There's a great Paul Krugman column

387
00:15:30,120 --> 00:15:31,640
where he goes and tries to explain it.

388
00:15:31,640 --> 00:15:34,280
Because what, of course, is the part of Obamacare

389
00:15:34,280 --> 00:15:35,960
that almost everybody hates?

390
00:15:35,960 --> 00:15:36,720
The mandate.

391
00:15:36,720 --> 00:15:38,760
The mandate, which is the only part actually

392
00:15:38,760 --> 00:15:40,800
justified by adverse selection.

393
00:15:40,800 --> 00:15:42,080
So the part that actually has anything

394
00:15:42,080 --> 00:15:44,040
to do with the market failure that we're talking about

395
00:15:44,040 --> 00:15:45,800
is the part that most people hate.

396
00:15:45,800 --> 00:15:47,800
So there's actually a very good survey

397
00:15:47,800 --> 00:15:50,040
that breaks up the different components of Obamacare

398
00:15:50,040 --> 00:15:51,320
that are the popularity.

399
00:15:51,320 --> 00:15:54,680
And I think only like 30% of Americans support the mandate.

400
00:15:54,680 --> 00:15:58,120
All right, now, what are the other parts?

401
00:15:58,120 --> 00:16:00,520
So the parts that people like.

402
00:16:00,520 --> 00:16:04,160
All right, well, there's the ban on pre-existing conditions

403
00:16:04,160 --> 00:16:07,160
clauses.

404
00:16:07,160 --> 00:16:10,480
All right, and remember, so pre-existing conditions

405
00:16:10,480 --> 00:16:14,200
clause, this is a clause where an insurance company says,

406
00:16:14,200 --> 00:16:16,120
we don't pay for problems that you knew you had

407
00:16:16,120 --> 00:16:18,320
when you bought the insurance.

408
00:16:18,320 --> 00:16:19,840
Now, when you think about it, that's

409
00:16:19,840 --> 00:16:23,920
almost like banning the prevention of insurance fraud.

410
00:16:23,920 --> 00:16:24,420
Right?

411
00:16:24,420 --> 00:16:26,200
Because this is saying that it's like you.

412
00:16:26,200 --> 00:16:29,720
Well, you can't refuse to sell insurance to people

413
00:16:29,720 --> 00:16:33,320
just because they're already mortally ill.

414
00:16:33,320 --> 00:16:36,120
So this is a regulation that has something

415
00:16:36,120 --> 00:16:37,320
to do with adverse selection.

416
00:16:37,320 --> 00:16:40,160
Although, by itself, what is the connection

417
00:16:40,160 --> 00:16:41,600
between this and adverse selection?

418
00:16:41,600 --> 00:16:42,200
Means to force.

419
00:16:42,200 --> 00:16:43,000
Yes.

420
00:16:43,000 --> 00:16:44,480
So there's this part that actually

421
00:16:44,480 --> 00:16:45,760
is connected to the model.

422
00:16:45,760 --> 00:16:47,980
This part, where if you believe the model is actually

423
00:16:47,980 --> 00:16:49,960
intensifying the problem.

424
00:16:49,960 --> 00:16:55,040
And then finally, there's the third part,

425
00:16:55,040 --> 00:16:56,080
which also very popular.

426
00:16:56,080 --> 00:16:57,440
These are the subsidies.

427
00:16:57,440 --> 00:17:01,520
So this is the part where you say, well,

428
00:17:01,520 --> 00:17:03,200
once we mandate you have to have it,

429
00:17:03,200 --> 00:17:04,200
a lot of people can't afford it.

430
00:17:04,200 --> 00:17:05,620
So the subsidies are there to make

431
00:17:05,620 --> 00:17:07,360
sure everybody can afford it.

432
00:17:07,360 --> 00:17:11,120
And Krugman actually, very reasonably,

433
00:17:11,120 --> 00:17:16,320
goes and says that this is Obamacare here.

434
00:17:16,320 --> 00:17:18,720
And these are the three legs of Obamacare.

435
00:17:18,720 --> 00:17:20,560
It's a stool with three legs.

436
00:17:20,560 --> 00:17:23,640
And he needs all three legs to be stable.

437
00:17:23,640 --> 00:17:25,720
And the way that he explains it, well,

438
00:17:25,720 --> 00:17:29,720
once you ban pre-existing conditions clauses,

439
00:17:29,720 --> 00:17:32,440
and you can actually also think about this combined

440
00:17:32,440 --> 00:17:36,120
with people rates or pretty equal rates,

441
00:17:36,120 --> 00:17:39,400
not exactly equal, but think about this in general

442
00:17:39,400 --> 00:17:40,560
as rate regulation.

443
00:17:40,560 --> 00:17:43,160
So once you go and say that any person can get insurance,

444
00:17:43,160 --> 00:17:44,960
no matter what the state of their health is,

445
00:17:44,960 --> 00:17:47,000
and that you're not allowed to go and charge them

446
00:17:47,000 --> 00:17:49,240
much more money, then the obvious thing

447
00:17:49,240 --> 00:17:51,000
in terms of the adverse selection model,

448
00:17:51,000 --> 00:17:52,040
how will people respond?

449
00:17:54,560 --> 00:17:56,760
You can get insurance at the normal rate

450
00:17:56,760 --> 00:17:59,320
if you just wait around until you really, really need it.

451
00:17:59,320 --> 00:18:01,720
So adverse selection says that people will do what?

452
00:18:01,720 --> 00:18:02,220
Wait around.

453
00:18:02,220 --> 00:18:03,920
Wait around.

454
00:18:03,920 --> 00:18:09,360
So if you wait around, then the problem is that the market

455
00:18:09,360 --> 00:18:11,320
equilibrium will be certain high prices.

456
00:18:11,320 --> 00:18:13,360
So that's where Krugman says, well,

457
00:18:13,360 --> 00:18:15,480
once we've done this regulation, then we

458
00:18:15,480 --> 00:18:17,320
got to do that regulation to go and solve

459
00:18:17,320 --> 00:18:19,280
the problems created by the first regulation.

460
00:18:19,280 --> 00:18:21,860
And then we need this regulation to solve the problems created

461
00:18:21,860 --> 00:18:24,120
by the other regulation.

462
00:18:24,120 --> 00:18:27,280
And it is a reasonable package.

463
00:18:27,280 --> 00:18:29,160
So I like the case for it.

464
00:18:29,160 --> 00:18:30,720
It's a very clear case.

465
00:18:30,720 --> 00:18:33,840
Although to go and say that Obamacare is there

466
00:18:33,840 --> 00:18:38,000
because the free market doesn't work is a gross injustice.

467
00:18:38,000 --> 00:18:39,720
Because he said, well, how about we just

468
00:18:39,720 --> 00:18:42,760
have none of these regulations?

469
00:18:42,760 --> 00:18:45,280
Then would there still be a problem?

470
00:18:45,280 --> 00:18:48,360
In particular, there's something really weird about this one.

471
00:18:48,360 --> 00:18:50,800
Because if you believe that insurance companies couldn't

472
00:18:50,800 --> 00:18:53,640
figure out who was sick and who was healthy,

473
00:18:53,640 --> 00:18:56,480
would there be any point of that regulation?

474
00:18:56,480 --> 00:18:57,360
Yeah.

475
00:18:57,360 --> 00:18:58,720
Why do you have that regulation?

476
00:18:58,720 --> 00:19:03,040
Because insurance companies do know.

477
00:19:03,040 --> 00:19:05,200
Insurance companies have armies of actuaries

478
00:19:05,200 --> 00:19:07,120
to do the run of statistics to figure out

479
00:19:07,120 --> 00:19:08,520
what your health status is.

480
00:19:08,520 --> 00:19:09,880
They've got lots of information.

481
00:19:09,880 --> 00:19:10,720
They can do tests.

482
00:19:10,720 --> 00:19:11,760
They can use your age.

483
00:19:11,760 --> 00:19:13,560
They can use past expenses.

484
00:19:13,560 --> 00:19:16,040
There's tons of stuff that they can do in order to figure it

485
00:19:16,040 --> 00:19:16,600
out.

486
00:19:16,600 --> 00:19:19,280
So this is the regulation that, at minimum,

487
00:19:19,280 --> 00:19:21,760
it makes the adverse selection problem worse.

488
00:19:21,760 --> 00:19:23,480
And then tries to, at first, makes it worse

489
00:19:23,480 --> 00:19:25,040
and then solves it another way.

490
00:19:25,040 --> 00:19:27,400
But it's also a kind of confession of guilt

491
00:19:27,400 --> 00:19:29,040
and saying, all right, fine.

492
00:19:29,040 --> 00:19:32,520
It's actually not the case that insurance companies don't

493
00:19:32,520 --> 00:19:33,400
know what's going on.

494
00:19:33,400 --> 00:19:34,680
Insurance companies know a lot.

495
00:19:34,680 --> 00:19:36,440
That's why they're going to go and charge sick people more

496
00:19:36,440 --> 00:19:39,080
rates, which would solve the problem we're actually

497
00:19:39,080 --> 00:19:40,560
talking about.

498
00:19:40,560 --> 00:19:43,840
But that's not our actual complaint.

499
00:19:43,840 --> 00:19:46,080
And then this brings you back into the normal world

500
00:19:46,080 --> 00:19:47,800
that has nothing to do with economics.

501
00:19:47,800 --> 00:19:51,840
What does a normal person want to go and require

502
00:19:51,840 --> 00:19:54,880
either ban pre-existing conditions clauses

503
00:19:54,880 --> 00:19:56,800
and require equal rates?

504
00:19:56,800 --> 00:19:59,320
Normal, economically illiterate person.

505
00:19:59,320 --> 00:20:00,800
They find it unreasonable.


